@@ -0,0 +1,129 @@
+# Experimental kernel read cache for Henge 0.2
+
+Base: release tag `0.2`, commit `f9c12c93620af4c4c5f5f65f9793b72f883be682`.
+Target for eventual device validation: M4 iPad Pro, 16 GB, iPadOS 27.2,
+iLoader installation with increased-memory-limit entitlement.
+
+This is an opt-in LUT fastmem prototype, **not** a complete host virtual-memory
+remapping backend. It has not been built with Xcode or booted on an iPad. No
+performance improvement has been measured. It may be slower than the release.
+
+## Behavior
+
+Settings > Performance > Kernel read cache (experimental) enables `HV_FASTMEM=1`
+before the next Mac starts. It defaults to off. Documents/henge-env.txt can
+override it with `HV_FASTMEM=0` or `HV_FASTMEM=1`, as with existing tuning options.
+Quit and reopen Henge before comparing runs; Henge permits one guest per launch.
+
+Each configured virtual CPU gets two page-indexed read lookup tables for a
+4 GB guest kernel window, anchored on its first eligible canonical kernel data
+access. One table handles normal EL1 reads, the other EL1 reads with PAN set.
+The arrays and sparse invalidation list reserve approximately 6 MiB per vCPU.
+They point into existing guest RAM; they do not allocate another copy of it.
+
+Ordinary Henge TLB entries collide for pages 256 MB apart. Pages in this LUT
+do not collide within its bounded window. The extra runtime guards make each
+lookup longer, so a workload needs enough avoided TLB misses to benefit.
+The first selected window may have poor coverage of a particular macOS boot.
+
+Only ungrouped scalar loads up to 8 bytes use the new JIT lookup. The ordinary
+walker must first establish a readable normal-memory RAM mapping with a guest
+page at least 16 KB. EL0/unprivileged accesses, stores, atomics, vectors,
+writeback instructions, tagged addresses, and out-of-window addresses use the
+existing path. The final 15 bytes of each page also use the existing path.
+There are no new Mach mappings, fault handlers or signing entitlements.
+
+Full TLB flushes clear populated LUT entries. Kernel page/range invalidations
+drop both permission views of overlapping pages. Kernel invalidations initiated
+by another vCPU use Henge's posted request/acknowledgement path so the owner
+invalidates before resuming. Existing write guards for translated code remain
+authoritative because stores never use this cache. Allocation failure leaves
+the CPU on the ordinary TLB path.
+
+## Checks performed in the Linux workspace
+
+- `sh tests/fastmem_portable.sh`: real walker and invalidation paths, with
+  AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer. Checks RAM remapping,
+  permissions/PAN, device and missing physical memory, 4 KB guest pages,
+  MMU-off/EL0 behavior, page ends, independent colliding pages, invalidations,
+  CPU reset, and saturation of the sparse clear list.
+- `python tests/test_fastmem_jit.py` with `unicorn` and `capstone`: 245 actual
+  emitted ARM64 memory cases, covering signed/unsigned loads, page ends,
+  fallback/disabled paths, PAN, register aliases, and reserved guest registers.
+  The test substitutes only the unrelated host DCZID probe when compiling the
+  emitter on Linux. It executes scalar inline code, not Henge's full dispatcher
+  or helper assembly. NZCV and unaffected registers are checked.
+- GCC syntax checks for the modified C sources and the CPU layout assertions;
+  the machine syntax check used a declaration-only Mach time header.
+
+## Build and device checks still required
+
+Apply the patch to the exact base above (`git apply --check`, then `git apply`).
+On an ARM64 Mac with the project's dependencies installed, run `make test` and
+`make test-fastmem`, then follow `ios/README.md` to build and package the app.
+PR #27 supplies the existing GitHub Actions build recipe if a Mac is unavailable;
+it needs to run from a fork containing this patch. A separate build request in the private Sideos5 repository applies the combined
+patch to this exact base and runs native checks before packaging. Build results
+and artifacts are recorded by that Actions run.
+
+Preserve an export of the guest before installing a test build. Compare the same
+guest, RAM allocation, vCPU count, code-cache size and workload with the switch
+off and on. Keep other experimental settings fixed. Alternate off/on launches
+and measure boot to desktop, a repeatable app launch, and responsiveness. Compare
+logs for guest faults and crashes. Include a multi-vCPU stress run that exercises
+mapping changes and permissions. Repeat on the actual iLoader-signed build:
+the portable and emulated tests do not establish iPad correctness.
+
+Ship enabled only if native tests, repeated device boots and integrity checks
+pass and paired measurements show a benefit. A full remapping fastmem backend,
+broader address coverage and write acceleration are separate follow-up work.
+
+
+## Combined custom build
+
+PR #31 (`21a0cb37b854a4b5d320444ee8cb2b9cbf4668e3`) is included unchanged:
+actual firmware disk capacity, bounded 128 MiB syscalls for larger requests,
+and EINTR/short-transfer handling. Its device regression tests are retained.
+`tests/disk_portable.sh` adds injected-syscall sanitizer checks.
+
+PR #14 (`48ffa577224fe6a16e8ab3588e3061e0a0db0b6b`) is ported to the current
+Settings and Trackpad files. The scroll setting defaults to 0.2 and wheel events
+are limited to 60 Hz. Three-finger controls and the newer pointer handling remain.
+Neither PR adds network destinations, signing entitlements, or secret access.
+The experimental kernel read cache remains disabled by default.
+
+
+## Diagnostic build revision
+
+Settings > Performance > Cache diagnostic counters now defaults off for everyday
+use, independently of the cache switch. Explicit saved choices are preserved. `HV_FASTMEM_STATS=0` in
+Documents/henge-env.txt can override it. Restart Henge after changing either
+switch. The counter increments are completely omitted from generated code when
+this diagnostic option is off. The normal guard itself is one ARM64 instruction
+shorter, using TBNZ for the page-end check; its boundary behavior is unchanged.
+
+Every ten seconds the native log reports `fastmem: mode=... counters=...`, then
+per-vCPU lifetime attempts, actual hits, hit percentage, fallback reasons,
+window base, fills, invalidations and sparse-list saturation. The JIT increments
+hits only when it supplies the address for the subsequent scalar load. Attempts
+include retries. These live snapshots are taken without stopping the other
+vCPUs; fields can advance slightly between reads. Hits establish use, not a
+speed improvement: disable diagnostics for controlled timing comparisons.
+
+The combined custom-source SHA and PR list are now printed at app launch. The
+firmware disk backend prints actual sector capacities and confirms the larger
+transfer path at initialization; PR #31 has no new UI setting.
+
+Magic Keyboard/mouse scroll events now use the separate `HardwareScroll` policy:
+callbacks are coalesced on a 60 Hz main-run-loop timer, capped at eight units per
+axis/event, with bounded pending motion and fractional retention. The default
+speed is 0.15 and the slider reaches 0.02. Existing explicit speed preferences
+are retained; the cap and revised conversion still apply. On-screen scrolling
+remains controlled by PR #14's separate slider. Pure Swift policy tests cover
+fractional accumulation, stale-axis callbacks, caps, direction and invalid values.
+
+Safari was initially reported as very slow, then returned to normal after macOS
+finished starting up. Networking code is unchanged in this revision. The release
+notes also list browsing trouble for Golden Gate, but that does not establish the
+cause on this iPad. If it recurs, compare the same page with the cache off/on after
+startup has settled before attributing it to the experimental cache.
