+# Direct USB-C feasibility for Henge Link
+
+Checked 8 November 2026 against the Henge Link source at 655d834 and current
+Apple documentation. No physical iPad Pro M4 / iPhone Duo pair is attached.
+
+## Result
+
+A supported, provisionable single-cable app-to-app USB transport has not been
+established for the existing iLoader-signed Henge installation. No direct-USB
+implementation or new USB-enabled IPA is being claimed. No restricted USB or
+DriverKit entitlement has been inserted into the app.
+
+The connector and hardware bandwidth are not the missing part. Henge needs an
+OS-accessible transport between the two app processes. Network.framework uses
+existing network interfaces; it does not create a USB device function, select a
+USB configuration, take ownership of USB endpoints, or grant raw USB access.
+If iPadOS independently creates an accessible IP interface for this particular
+cable/device pair, ordinary networking could use it, but that has not been
+verified and is not established by charging or migration support.
+
+## Confirmed source boundary
+
+* host/libusb_shim.c explicitly implements one virtual device: the emulated
+  Mac's restore USB port. Its g_usb is an HvVirtioUsb pointer. It is not libusb
+  access to the iPad's physical connector.
+* host/usb_host.c operates on HvVirtioUsb messages and endpoints. Naming it a
+  USB host does not make the underlying device physical.
+* host/install.c starts a private usbmuxd for the virtual restore path. Bundling
+  libusbmuxd/libimobiledevice does not provide the missing physical USB backend.
+* ios/Henge/Link/LinkTransport.swift uses Network.framework TCP/TLS. Its wired
+  policy selects an OS Ethernet interface; it does not implement a raw USB link.
+* Neither Henge signing-entitlements file requests DriverKit. The delivered
+  IPA has its original JIT helper, not an authorized USB DriverKit extension.
+
+## Current Apple platform evidence
+
+IOUSBHost documents macOS 10.15 and Mac Catalyst 14 availability. Its public
+module is not documented for ordinary iOS/iPadOS apps:
+https://developer.apple.com/documentation/iousbhost
+
+USBDriverKit is supported on M-series iPads. An M4 iPad is eligible hardware;
+this does not prove this developer team can provision a driver for Apple's
+phone USB interfaces:
+https://developer.apple.com/documentation/driverkit/creating-drivers-for-ipados
+https://developer.apple.com/documentation/usbdriverkit
+
+Apple requires a valid DriverKit entitlement set and matching code-signing /
+provisioning authorization. The request includes transport and hardware vendor
+information; Apple approval is not supplied by an iLoader RAM entitlement:
+https://developer.apple.com/documentation/driverkit/requesting-entitlements-for-driverkit-development
+https://developer.apple.com/documentation/bundleresources/entitlements/com.apple.developer.driverkit
+https://developer.apple.com/documentation/bundleresources/entitlements/com.apple.developer.driverkit.communicates-with-drivers
+
+A possible longer-term driver design would run on the M4 iPad and communicate
+with the phone's existing USB service (such as usbmux). It does not necessarily
+require the phone to run DriverKit. Actual endpoint ownership, phone trust /
+pairing, provisioning and hardware transfer tests remain prerequisites. Apple
+may not grant the requested access; no approval is implied.
+
+External Accessory is an MFi accessory/protocol API, not a general direct-iPhone
+USB socket. AVFoundation external UVC capture similarly consumes an OS-supported
+camera device; it does not make another ordinary iOS app emulate a USB camera:
+https://developer.apple.com/documentation/externalaccessory
+https://developer.apple.com/documentation/externalaccessory/easession
+https://developer.apple.com/documentation/avfoundation/avcapturedevice/devicetype-swift.struct/external
+
+Apple's USB Personal Hotspot instructions describe Mac or Windows clients, not
+a guaranteed direct iPad-to-iPhone connection:
+https://support.apple.com/guide/iphone/share-your-internet-connection-iph45447ca6/ios
+
+Current JSON documentation responses are retained alongside this report. Apple
+DTS thread 802640 redirected to a human-verification page on this pass; its
+current contents were not treated as newly verified evidence.
+
+## Other USB implementations reviewed
+
+The virtual-screen project's NCM configuration uses a Linux USB host,
+libusb/usbmuxd and NetworkManager, not an iPad-only USB host implementation:
+https://github.com/hkfuertes/virtual-screen
+
+A computer can be the USB host for both Apple devices using usbmux. Making
+Henge Link use that arrangement would require a real desktop relay and a viewer
+inbound/loopback-proxy mode; the current client/server directions cannot be
+relayed by merely plugging in cables. An opaque relay could preserve end-to-end
+TLS rather than terminate encryption on the computer. This is a separate design
+requiring a Mac/PC, two data cables, device trust and physical validation. It is
+not a single direct cable and has not been implemented or advertised as ready.
+
+The user explicitly requires one direct USB-C cable, with no computer. A relay
+is therefore outside the requested solution and has not been implemented.
+The existing Wi-Fi / compatible-Ethernet version remains available.
