+# Second-display startup assertion
+
+The user's 8 November 2026 log identifies build request 0ebb453b, successful
+TLS pairing and hardware HEVC setup at 2853×2007/120 Hz, then a guest panic:
+`"hitting assertion" @AppleParavirtDisplay.cpp:314`. Both display state pages
+were registered, but there was no port-1 mode acknowledgement or first swap.
+The Duo's disconnect was a consequence of the guest halt.
+
+The exact guest kernel was obtained from Apple's macOS 27.2 / 26B123 restore
+image using HTTP ranges for its 23 MB kernel entry, not a full restore download.
+The decoded fileset UUID matches the log:
+B600F3DF-E705-391F-3543-16E4669AE151. The driver UUID also matches:
+7EC9457F-5EF0-3257-983D-F651D9E54691.
+
+`AppleParavirtDisplay::setupSharedState` reads the 16-bit field at shared-page
++0x12 and compares it with the requested port. The diagnostic condition string
+is `fSharedState->port == fPort`. A mismatch branches to the line-314 assertion.
+The old custom publisher left this field zero on both pages: valid for primary
+port zero, invalid for secondary port one.
+
+The publisher now writes the actual port before the release publication of the
+mode flag. Port identity is initialized both at board creation and command
+registration, and remains correct across secondary disconnect publication.
+The regression checks reproduce the guest's actual precondition, including a
+negative case for the old zero-filled secondary field, and test real production
+per-port command routing. The Duo's native 2853×2007 geometry is also included
+in the hardware codec tests to check the next stage at the actual reported size.
+No Retina dimensions, CPU count, RAM choice or selected refresh rate are reduced.
+
+This source diagnosis confirms why the reported assertion was taken. The Mac
+host reference-device ring probe did not initialize display pages and is not
+counted as a successful guest or reference-state validation. Full macOS boot,
+mode acknowledgement and first extended frame still require the user's device
+pair. No Mac installation or user disk is edited by this fix.
+
+## Native odd-sized video canvas
+
+Testing the user's exact 2853×2007 mode exposed a second issue after the guest
+assertion: hardware codecs use a chroma-compatible even canvas. The old receiver
+compared its encoded dimensions directly to the odd negotiated viewport and
+rejected it. The sender now snapshots the native image 1:1 into the top-left of
+an even canvas, clears the extra right/bottom pixels, and explicitly transmits
+both canvas and visible dimensions. The receiver checks both against strict
+bounds and supplies a clean-aperture attachment to Core Media for presentation.
+It does not stretch the extra pixels over the screen. Tests inspect the actual
+Core Media aperture, presentation dimensions, RGB/cursor and half-float output.
+Both devices must receive the updated app for this odd-sized mode.
+
+Native test run 42518156939 confirmed port identity/ASan command routing,
+protocol bounds, TLS recovery and repeated hardware frames at even sizes. Its
+odd-size test caught a clean-aperture offset error, corrected in the source.
+The corrected run 42518539768 failed before any runner steps, including one
+retry, and produced no logs; it is not counted as passing validation. Final
+native build validation must confirm the corrected aperture before delivery.
