@@ -0,0 +1,88 @@
+# Henge iOS performance review — 7 November 2026
+
+Scope: the pinned Henge 0.2 source and custom changes, covering scheduling,
+translation/cache lifetime, input, logging, graphics, memory, networking and
+startup. This is a targeted review, not an exhaustive review of all iOS coding,
+a physical-device profile, or a measured M4 power/temperature result.
+
+## Changes in this revision
+
+1. **Native log mirroring becomes event driven.** HengeLog previously read a
+   regular file to EOF, slept 40 ms and repeated on a user-initiated thread.
+   NativeLogTail now registers a filesystem write source on a utility queue,
+   drains complete lines on writes and performs an initial drain to catch bytes
+   predating registration. Raw stderr remains an unbuffered file; crash capture
+   does not depend on the mirror queue. Tests cover preexisting bytes, partial
+   UTF-8 lines, a 1,000-line burst, cancellation and no repeated drains at idle.
+2. **Hardware scroll timers exist only while whole wheel units are pending.**
+   The previous 60 Hz repeating timer ran throughout a session even without a
+   mouse or gesture. One-shot scheduling now coalesces callbacks, drains the
+   bounded tail and stops. Fractional motion survives without a timer; later
+   events resume delivery. Disconnect and stop cancel pending delivery. The
+   per-event cap and speed calculation are preserved. Boundary tests include
+   fractional accumulation and idle eligibility. Physical trackpad feel remains
+   a device check, especially given the user's guest reinstall fixed scrolling.
+3. **The VM kicker stops with the guest.** host/vm.c had an unconditional
+   lifetime loop sleeping 1 ms and kicking all CPUs. A generation token now
+   cancels it on CPU0 return or explicit stop, and the worker is detached.
+   A new start cannot revive an old sleeping worker. A threaded test exercises
+   stop/restart while the old worker waits. A kick already in flight may finish.
+   Running-guest cadence stays 1 ms: this change targets post-stop background
+   work, not a claimed reduction of all active guest timer activity.
+4. **Pipeline creation is measured.** GPU reports accumulate creation count,
+   elapsed time and worst creation duration for guest render/tile/compute
+   pipeline builds, including failed attempts. This measures calls to Metal;
+   it does not attribute the time solely to shader compilation. Emission is
+   part of the existing periodic GPU report, not a new polling loop.
+
+Apple recommends filesystem notifications instead of periodic checks and
+cancelling timers once no work remains. That directly supports changes 1–3;
+it does not quantify their impact in this emulator.
+[Apple: Minimize Timer Use](https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/Performance/Conceptual/EnergyGuide-iOS/MinimizeTimerUse.html)
+
+Log mirroring is noninteractive support work, so a utility queue is appropriate;
+the synchronous raw file preserves crash evidence. VM execution remains
+user-initiated rather than indiscriminately raising all work to interactive QoS.
+[Apple: Prioritize Work with QoS](https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/Performance/Conceptual/EnergyGuide-iOS/PrioritizeWorkWithQoS.html)
+
+## Other paths examined
+
+| Area | Henge-specific finding | Decision / next evidence |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Running guest timer | 1 ms kicker makes CPUs leave translated code; CPU0 polls devices, other CPUs update their own timers. WFI also uses deadlines. | A deadline-driven replacement needs correct guest timer/sysreg changes, device wakeups and interrupt-latency tests. Blindly slowing the ticker could worsen input/audio/network latency. |
+| JIT | CPU code starts empty; full resets discard links and translated blocks. Previous revision skips some duplicate placements. | Hot-block retention requires allocator and link lifetime changes. Per-device dedup/flush logs show whether this workload benefits. Raw disk persistence is unsafe without relocation/validation. |
+| GPU presentation | Presenter already stops when screen_dirty is false and coalesces newer frames while waiting for a drawable. | Preserve this behavior. Guest completion stamps constrain command-buffer merging. Capture Metal System Trace before restructuring submission. |
+| Shader startup | makeSerialized creates guest render/tile/compute pipelines synchronously. | New timing identifies actual stalls. Binary archives are a candidate, but private descriptor reconstruction, GPU/OS compatibility, cache invalidation and render correctness must be validated first. No persistent shader cache added here. |
+| Networking | libslirp blocks on poll, sockets, a wake pipe and its timer deadlines. | No evidence that arbitrary longer sleeps would help; preserve Safari/network behavior. |
+| Disk | Virtio block requests use worker threads and condition variables. A 100 µs drain poll is only used by tests. | Do not describe changing a test-only helper as an app performance gain. |
+| Memory | Large guest allocations, JIT storage and graphics share device resources; entitlement limits are not a promise of spare physical RAM. | Do not increase guest/JIT allocations blindly. Compare footprint and macOS memory pressure before changing capacity. |
+| UI/background | Session stats update once a second; zoom following is a separate task. | Further UI isolation and lifecycle suspension need traces and external-display/background-use checks; avoid pausing a guest unexpectedly. |
+
+Metal command-buffer guidance calls for balancing submission overhead against GPU
+starvation; this emulator must additionally honor the guest's completion protocol.
+[Apple: Command Buffers](https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/3DDrawing/Conceptual/MTLBestPracticesGuide/CommandBuffers.html)
+
+Apple's binary archives can reduce pipeline compilation work. Their existence
+makes shader persistence worth investigating, but does not establish compatibility
+with Henge's reconstructed guest descriptors or guarantee faster launches.
+[Apple: Metal Binary Archives](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/metal/metal-binary-archives)
+
+App memory limits can differ from available physical memory; large use can affect
+system performance even below the app's limit.
+[Apple: os_proc_available_memory](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/os/os_proc_available_memory)
+
+## Startup and measurement
+
+The prior device log supports JIT cache turnover during startup, but cannot name
+macOS background processes or identify all launch stalls. Pipeline timing adds
+another concrete signal. No instant warm startup or temperature reduction is
+claimed. Compare identical guest state/settings and workloads across builds:
+first app launch, repeat launch, desktop idle, and Henge left open after stopping
+the guest. Keep charging state and display brightness comparable. The revised
+log reader and scroll scheduling remove periodic callbacks even if the VM's
+other activity prevents deep sleep; callback counts are not energy measurements.
+
+Time Profiler and Power Profiler on the physical device are the appropriate next
+measurements for CPU/power attribution, alongside the new logs. Apple notes that
+Power Profiler measures system power, so unrelated device activity needs control.
+[Apple: Measuring Power Use](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/xcode/measuring-your-app-s-power-use-with-power-profiler)
