+# Responsiveness and startup revision
+
+This revision removes specific avoidable work. It does not establish a measured
+app-launch speedup, lower iPad temperature, or instant warm startup.
+
+* After parallel code emission, recheck a changed block-hash bucket before copying
+  the result into executable memory. If another CPU published the exact VA/PA,
+  flags and page-generation identity, reuse it. This avoids that copy, cache
+  maintenance and hot/cold code allocation. Parallel emission remains unlocked.
+  The original locked publication check handles later races. Emission and exit
+  slot allocation still occur; this is not complete duplicate-work elimination.
+* GPU command batches cache the trace-file existence result for one second on the
+  serial worker. No timer wakes an idle GPU. Trace toggles are observed on the next
+  command after the interval. A test drives 2,000 batches in a second and observes
+  one filesystem probe instead of 2,000, including enable/disable checks.
+* Detailed fastmem instruction counters default off. Explicit saved preferences
+  and henge-env.txt still win. Normal cache and performance logs continue. For
+  device timing, confirm fastmem log says counters=off.
+
+## Cold starts
+
+JIT code and memory-translation caches start empty on a new launch. macOS/Safari
+also perform their own disk-cache loading and background work. The provided
+six-vCPU, 1.5 GiB-code-cache log shows a nearly full code region at guest 32s and
+69s, with full flush counts increasing afterward and substantial approximate
+retranslation. This supports cache turnover as a contributor to pauses, without
+identifying every startup delay. The log does not identify Spotlight or prove a
+thermal slowdown (thermal was Normal).
+
+The dispatcher discards all translations on a full reset. A durable executable
+cache cannot safely reuse raw bytes across launch: runtime addresses, mappings,
+links and code generations need validation/relocation. This revision does not
+implement persistent JIT storage or precompile the guest. Retaining hot blocks
+across capacity resets is a larger allocator/link-invalidation project.
+
+## What to collect
+
+Boot logging identifies this revision. Ten-second reports include guest elapsed
+time, JIT usage, work and reset causes, and dedup counts/avoided code bytes. GPU
+reports include actual trace probes and cached checks. Counters are cumulative;
+capacity reset requests can coalesce across CPUs. Approximate retranslations use
+existing hashed statistics and can overcount. Stats-dependent work fields require
+HV_STATS > 0 (default 10). These are activity metrics, not speedup measurements.
+
+Compare identical guest snapshots/settings and the same app-launch sequence over
+several alternating baseline/new runs. Record first launch and repeat launch,
+idle temperature/thermal state and battery conditions. Avoid changing CPU count,
+resolution or experimental features during comparison. The native CI suite
+covers JIT execution and forced-small-cache resets; a deterministic publication
+race test checks reuse, late races, identity and generation rejection. Portable
+GPU trace-policy tests and ARM64 emitted-memory tests supplement it. No physical
+M4 profile or guest process trace was available in the build workspace.
