+# Scroll and idle efficiency revision — 8 November 2026
+
+## Scrolling
+
+The former hardware path took sqrt of each callback's delta, then summed converted
+wheel units. Splitting one raw movement into N callbacks could increase the
+uncapped output by sqrt(N). It also compared paired-axis values to previous values,
+which could suppress legitimate repeated deltas and replay the other axis.
+
+The new path uses individual axis callbacks and batches raw movement before a
+single conversion per delivery (up to 60 Hz). Hardware gain is reduced from 16 to
+8; both hardware and on-screen output are bounded to four units per axis/event,
+instead of the former hardware bound of eight and unbounded on-screen output.
+Capped motion is consumed rather than queued as a long tail. Fractional units
+persist without an idle timer. Scroll preferences are cached and updated by
+UserDefaults notifications rather than queried for every hardware callback.
+Reset buttons restore sensible speeds without reinstalling macOS.
+
+On-screen movement now uses a cached centroid of all fingers. The old path read
+only the first finger's previousLocation, even when a callback concerned the other
+finger. Cached positions prevent replaying that old delta. Membership changes
+reset the baseline; cancellation drops queued scroll and cannot generate a click.
+Three-finger control gestures cancel pending wheel delivery. Tap classification
+uses actual contact travel so opposite finger movement cannot masquerade as a tap.
+Any cancelled contact suppresses the gesture’s later click. Hardware input is suspended while
+Henge's panels are open, preventing the guest scrolling behind a settings sheet.
+
+These tests establish policy invariants, not identical physical gesture feel on
+every mouse/trackpad or macOS application's acceleration curve. Axis orientation
+retains the existing device mapping; a physical M4 input check remains necessary.
+
+## Quiet guest ticker
+
+The host ticker formerly slept 1 ms and kicked all CPUs throughout a running VM.
+Now it waits on a condition variable when all CPUs are parked. Publishing CPU
+activity wakes it; the waiter is armed before checking CPU flags to close the
+lost-wakeup window. Generations prevent an old worker being revived by stop/start.
+The active cadence remains 1 ms; WFI deadlines, device wakeups, invalidations and
+stop-the-world behavior retain their existing paths. A running or in-place WFE
+CPU is active, so this does not promise savings during a busy macOS workload.
+
+`HV_IDLE_TICKER=0` in henge-env.txt selects the original active polling behavior for
+comparison. The default is on. Existing ten-second logging now reports ticker
+ticks/waits/resumes and wheel callbacks/delivered frames/max units. Those are
+activity counters, not measured energy savings. The same report also reads
+getrusage(RUSAGE_SELF) for cumulative process user/system CPU milliseconds, so
+paired device logs can compare CPU-time deltas without requiring Instruments.
+This is CPU usage, not GPU energy or a temperature sensor. No new diagnostic timer is added.
+
+## Precise memory checks at page tails
+
+The existing TLB guard conservatively rejects the last 15 bytes of a page even
+when a smaller access fits. In the supplied device log, the page-end category
+was a substantial share of slow memory traffic. HV_RECHECK=1 enables the existing
+out-of-line exact check, allowing a cached, permitted, entirely in-page access
+back to the generated load/store without the full C helper. Actual crossings,
+invalid tags, MMIO and code-page writes retain their fallback. The normal hot
+path is unchanged; six words are added to applicable cold paths for accesses smaller than 16 bytes,
+so code-cache
+pressure can increase. This is not persistent caching or a permission bypass.
+
+Sixteen-byte accesses already have an exact guard and are excluded from the
+extra cold-path check; the native benchmark asserts no code-size growth for them.
+The default iOS tuning now selects this check; HV_RECHECK=0 is the comparison
+mode. A native benchmark compares middle-of-page, valid-last-eight-byte and real
+cross-page loads with identical guest results, recording helper calls, emitted
+code words and time. Native memory/device tests also run with the recheck enabled,
+including its combination with experimental fastmem. Native machine creation now
+loads environment knobs consistently with the iOS JIT-attachment entry point;
+previously native tests could silently leave the recheck knob off.
+
+## Verification and limits
+
+Tests cover callback partitions with equal raw totals, repeated deltas, malformed
+values, bounds, reversal, fractional accumulation, no queued tail, contact
+centroids and membership transitions. A threaded test exercises 1,000 activation
+races, quiet waiting without recurring checks, cancellation and stale generations;
+ASan/UBSan run locally. Native CI also executes WFI guest code and verifies that
+the activity callback runs on guest entry and resumption and that code continues.
+Existing MMU, JIT, devices and emitted ARM64-memory regressions remain in place.
+
+Further large improvements (hot-code retention, shader archives, scheduler/MMU
+work) require representative physical-device traces. The provided older log shows
+cache resets, but no trace of the new ticker or new scroll path. No significant
+whole-system temperature/FPS/launch-time claim can be established in this Linux
+workspace or by a native build test. Compare identical guest snapshots, RAM,
+CPU count, screen mode, brightness and charging conditions on the M4 iPad.
+
+Native ARM64 CI result: the targeted 100,000-load loop used 100,000 full helpers
+with the conservative guard and one helper with the precise check. A real page
+crossing used 100,000 helpers in both modes; guest results matched. The loop's
+emitted code grew from 43 to 49 words. This isolates the mechanism and is not an
+M4 whole-application speedup or energy measurement.
