@@ -0,0 +1,189 @@
+Henge 0.2 security and privacy review — 7 November 2026
+
+This is a scoped source review and local regression testing, not a security
+certification. Emulation is local, but the app is not fully offline. I found no
+account-data upload feature, telemetry SDK, or author-operated login backend in
+the Henge application code inspected. That does not prove the absence of such
+behavior in prebuilt dependencies or in the installed guest and VPN applications.
+I would use noncritical test accounts rather than primary email, banking,
+password-manager, recovery, or work credentials in this experimental build.
+
+The emulator and its native helpers can access guest memory and disks by design.
+HTTPS inside Safari protects traffic in transit to websites; it does not protect
+credentials from a compromised emulator, guest OS, or native dependency.
+
+Review scope and evidence
+
+- Henge release `0.2`, commit `f9c12c93620af4c4c5f5f65f9793b72f883be682`.
+- Custom PR #14 / #31 and kernel-read-cache build through Sideos5 commit
+  `516e1ede3daee2fd70723835936a2e5ce136fb04`, plus the changes described below.
+- Pinned Husk dependency commit `afe83d45b31194c4cc9f7b3db7ec2c757db8346e`:
+  on-device pairing wrapper, dependency/build scripts, and StikJIT's public
+  interface and binary strings. The prebuilt StikJIT framework was not rebuilt
+  from independently matched source or exhaustively reverse engineered.
+- Reviewed installation/download paths, network NAT, folder and clipboard
+  sharing, pairing-record storage, app/extension entry points, JIT lifecycle,
+  logging, build packaging, and selected guest-memory/device boundaries.
+- No packet capture or physical-device penetration test was performed. This
+  cannot enumerate all connections made by the installed macOS, Safari,
+  iLoader, LocalDevVPN, or another sideloader. There was no exhaustive audit of
+  all vendored C/Rust code, a complete dependency-vulnerability database scan,
+  or a formal proof of guest isolation.
+
+Where computation and data go
+
+| Component / destination | Observed behavior | Evidence |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| CPU, MMU, guest RAM, graphics | Native on-device emulator/JIT and Metal rendering; no remote-desktop or cloud-compute service identified in the runtime paths reviewed. | `core/dispatch.c`, `core/translate.c`, `host/vm.c`, `devices/pvg_metal.m` |
+| Guest disks | Local `Application Support/Machines/<UUID>/disk.img` and auxiliary storage. Machine storage is marked excluded from backups; exported copies are separate files controlled by the user. Henge does not add its own disk-encryption format. | `ios/Henge/Machines.swift` |
+| Apple restore downloads | HTTPS downloads from `updates.cdn-apple.com`; BootROM additionally checks an expected SHA-256. Restore personalization sends machine identifiers/nonces to Apple's TSS services. | `BootROM.swift`, `Installer.swift`, `host/install.c`, `third_party/libtatsu/src/tss.c` |
+| Developer image preparation | The bundled StikJIT binary contains `https://github.com/doronz88/DeveloperDiskImage/raw/refs/heads/main/PersonalizedImages/`. Its interface exposes developer-image downloads and preparation. This is a third-party external dependency, not a purely local operation. A binary string is evidence of an embedded address, not proof of every request made at runtime. | Husk's pinned `StikJIT.xcframework` binary and Swift interface |
+| JIT debugging | LocalDevVPN/device service address defaults to `10.7.0.1:49152`; mDNS discovers pairing services. The on-device pairing wrapper temporarily binds all IPv4 interfaces on an ephemeral port and uses the pairing protocol/PIN. The VPN application's own routing and privacy are outside this review. | `JITSetup.swift`, `JITPairing.swift`, `HengeJITHelper.swift`, Husk `src/rppairing-ios/src/lib.rs` |
+| Guest internet / DNS | libslirp NAT forwards the guest's network through the iPad's connection. `10.0.2.3` is its virtual DNS address; websites, DNS providers and the guest OS can receive ordinary network traffic. No author-operated mandatory proxy was identified in this path. | `host/net_slirp.c` |
+| Shared files and clipboard | Internal `10.0.2.4:80` is forwarded to a Unix-domain socket with mode 0600. This server is not a default public LAN TCP listener. It deliberately bridges guest apps to the Shared folder and clipboard operations. | `host/share.c`, `Sharing.swift`, `tools/henge-agent.m` |
+| Logs | App/native logs are local files and can be shared by the user's explicit Share action. No automatic Henge log-upload call was identified. Logs can still contain identifiers, paths, debug registers and guest serial output. | `HengeLog.swift`, `SettingsView.swift`, `host/vm.c` |
+| Build service | GitHub Actions builds source in the private Sideos5 repository. Personal Apple certificates and guest disks are not inputs to this build recipe; the user signs afterward. This is a build-time cloud service, not runtime off-device emulation. | `.github/workflows/henge-ipa.yml` on `ipa/henge` |
+
+Findings and changes
+
+1. **Confirmed guest-memory span overflow; hardened in this revision.**
+   `hv_phys_ptr` previously checked `pa + len - region_base > region_size`.
+   With an address eight bytes into RAM and `len = UINT64_MAX`, the arithmetic
+   wrapped and accepted the span. A local test of the actual source helpers
+   reproduced this failure. The check now compares length against remaining
+   region size without adding untrusted length. Tests cover valid endpoints,
+   oversized spans, and regions near the top of the 64-bit address range.
+   Native CI also links these tests against the actual engine. This establishes
+   an API bounds defect; no end-to-end Safari or remote guest exploit was
+   demonstrated. Exploitability depends on callers and other size limits.
+
+2. **Shared-folder containment has a conditional parent-symlink weakness.**
+   URL decoding rejects `..`, and GET uses `O_NOFOLLOW` on the final component.
+   However, `local_path` joins a string path beneath the root; a pre-existing
+   symlink in a parent directory can be followed. A local fixture confirmed
+   that `Shared/link/marker` can be opened outside Shared despite final-component
+   `O_NOFOLLOW`. Several other operations use string-based stat/rename/copy.
+   This requires a suitable host-side link or path race; the WebDAV interface
+   does not itself offer a symlink-creation method. Access remains constrained
+   by the iOS app sandbox, but that sandbox contains sensitive Henge data.
+   Full descriptor-relative containment hardening remains outstanding.
+   This revision provides an off switch for the guest folder/automatic clipboard
+   bridge; it does not claim to repair every shared-server path race.
+
+3. **Sharing is an intentional data boundary, not per-guest-app isolation.**
+   The internal WebDAV and clipboard endpoints have no per-application guest
+   authentication. Native apps in the guest can access them; browser-origin
+   policy may restrict a website, so that is not equivalent to proving arbitrary
+   web-page access. The helper forwards ordinary guest clipboard text to the
+   iPad, skipping text marked with `org.nspasteboard.ConcealedType`.
+   Device-to-guest paste is initiated explicitly by the user.
+   A new Settings > Guest sharing switch can disable the host server before a
+   session starts. Quit and reopen Henge to apply it. It does not erase files
+   already copied into the guest, and explicit Paste still types chosen text.
+
+4. **Pairing records need stronger storage hygiene.**
+   `JITPairingFileStore` keeps a private-key-bearing plist under
+   `Documents/StikJIT/pairingFile.plist`, visible through the app's Files/Finder
+   sharing. The helper creates an atomic temporary copy and normally deletes
+   it on completion. These paths do not explicitly request a file-protection
+   class, Keychain storage or backup exclusion. Existing iOS container/file
+   protection still applies; this is not a claim that the file is publicly
+   downloadable. Treat it as a sensitive device-development credential and do
+   not include it in support uploads. Moving it privately with a tested migration
+   and explicit export flow is recommended; this revision does not move it.
+
+5. **Prebuilt code and mutable developer-image downloads limit provenance.**
+   The workflow pins Henge/Husk commits, but StikJIT is copied as a prebuilt
+   framework. A pinned container repository does not prove its binary matches
+   independently reviewed source. The embedded developer-image URL references
+   a mutable `main` branch. Guest/native JIT helpers are trusted components with
+   access to memory and pairing data. Reproducible/source-matched framework
+   builds, dependency inventory/signature checks, and runtime egress evidence
+   are needed for a stronger assurance statement.
+
+6. **Restore transport deserves a separate hardening pass.**
+   Vendored restore code includes plain-HTTP TSS fallback URLs and calls that
+   disable libcurl certificate verification. In this iOS build, the custom curl
+   shim uses NSURLSession and ignores that disable-verification option; no
+   custom trust-all certificate challenge handler was found there. Therefore
+   the vendored option alone does not prove HTTPS validation is disabled in
+   this IPA. A plain-HTTP TSS address is also present in StikJIT binary strings;
+   its actual use could not be established from that inspection. Enforce HTTPS
+   and test restore/DDI preparation rather than assuming all transports are
+   encrypted. This does not describe Safari's independent HTTPS implementation.
+
+7. **Logs are sensitive even without automatic upload.**
+   Debug output can include guest identifiers and crash register data. The app
+   previously logged complete callback URLs, potentially including query tokens.
+   This revision records callback receipt/scheme without path/query contents.
+   New cache diagnostics contain counts, window addresses and cache usage, not
+   passwords, URLs, cookies or clipboard contents. Review any full log before
+   sharing it publicly. The absence of a telemetry SDK is not a general proof
+   about every embedded binary or the iOS system's own diagnostics settings.
+
+Practical login recommendation
+
+Use a test account with HTTPS and MFA for experimentation. For primary accounts,
+prefer a supported browser on the iPad or a better-established VM environment
+until dependency provenance and the remaining guest/host boundaries are reviewed
+and hardened. Keep Shared empty of sensitive files or disable its bridge, keep
+pairing records private, and treat VM exports as potentially containing cookies,
+sessions and account data. Logging into Safari does not bypass the need to trust
+Henge itself: the emulator necessarily sees the guest's execution and memory.
+
+Caching and performance
+
+The JIT code cache and experimental read LUT are RAM-only. Browser disk caches,
+cookies and macOS-installed files are separate and may persist on the guest disk.
+The JIT cache starts empty on app launch and may be reset when it fills. Keeping
+the same guest session alive can retain valid translations until invalidation or
+eviction, but it does not guarantee a warm browser after quitting the app.
+
+Saving raw executable cache bytes would be unsafe: emitted blocks contain host
+and guest mapping assumptions, block generations, code-page state, callbacks and
+cross-block links. A persistent cache would need version/guest-code validation,
+relocation, invalidation and a trusted cache-loading design. Precompiling every
+possible block is not practical; code is discovered dynamically and Safari can
+generate new code during execution. This revision adds explicit RAM-only lifecycle,
+cache occupancy and full-flush logs, not a pretend persistent-cache feature.
+
+The current non-speculative approach is to let macOS settle, preserve the same
+session, use the existing code-cache size settings, and compare the same workload
+with diagnostic counters off. Lower refresh rate/resolution or fewer guest vCPUs
+can be tested separately; no speed guarantee or automatic preference reset is
+made by this review.
+
+PR #32 integration addendum
+
+The native-touch helper at PR commit
+`fd30cda9ffb6f93cefdd6ae67480c90d61f10334` uses the existing local guest bridge
+`http://10.0.2.4/.henge/native-touch`; no new external service was identified.
+It sends bounded contact frames to a CoreHID virtual device inside macOS and
+requires guest Accessibility/virtual-HID authorization. The virtual-device
+entitlement is on the compressed guest helper, not added to Henge's iOS app.
+The feature defaults off and falls back to the trackpad. The shared-server
+boundary remains available to guest native applications without per-app auth;
+its existing trust limitations therefore also apply to touch data/status.
+The custom sharing off switch disables the server, and the native-touch setup
+controls explain this dependency. Contact bounds, duplicate IDs, generations,
+queue overflow and watchdog fallbacks are tested; this is not a physical-device
+security certification or a general performance improvement.
+
+Runtime correction — 8 November 2026: the user's guest AMFI logs confirm that
+PR32's ad-hoc helper is rejected with error -424 for restricted entitlements.
+Accessibility permission was separately granted successfully. The earlier
+packaging check established an embedded entitlement, not its authorization by
+macOS. Proper Mac entitlement provisioning is required; the iOS sideloader
+cannot grant it. Trackpad fallback is the supported path for this build under
+normal guest enforcement. No guest protections are disabled by these changes.
+
+Latest-main scope update — 8 November 2026
+
+The new base is main `ba9392e7ce13c6e80f2a635cf4a10336f27c0964`.
+Review of its 19-commit delta identified local testing automation in
+`Autopilot.swift`: it runs only with HENGE_AUTOPILOT or a one-time Documents
+plan file. It can type commands into the guest and change test knobs; do not
+import untrusted autopilot plans. Typed text is logged by length, although the
+plan itself remains plaintext on disk. No new external upload endpoint was
+identified in this delta. Other review limits and prebuilt-dependency concerns
+above still apply; this is not an exhaustive renewed security audit.
