+# Sustained performance research and implementation — 8 November 2026
+
+## Device evidence
+
+The successful a434cbdb log retains 2752x2064 Retina, four CPUs and a 1536 MiB JIT.
+It records nearly seven million GPU draw calls, zero reported GPU failures,
+six JIT capacity resets and iPadOS serious thermal pressure from about 140 guest
+seconds onward. The user prefers unchanged resolution and frame rate. No thermal
+or presentation cap is enabled by default in this revision.
+
+## Research applied
+
+- Apple's thermal-state guidance explicitly recommends reducing resource work
+  and optionally frame rate at serious heat. A frame cap is a power/quality
+  tradeoff, not an efficiency improvement or a guaranteed FPS boost. It is
+  explicit opt-in here; Retina and existing 60/120 Hz preferences are preserved.
+  https://developer.apple.com/documentation/foundation/processinfo/thermalstate-swift.enum/serious
+- Apple's timer guide recommends event-driven work over polling. Existing idle
+  waiting stays. Blindly slowing the emulator's 1 ms fallback timer could harm
+  device and interrupt latency, so this revision preserves it and optimizes the
+  interrupt computation it invokes instead.
+  https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/Performance/Conceptual/EnergyGuide-iOS/MinimizeTimerUse.html
+- Apple's Metal command-buffer guidance balances CPU submission against GPU
+  starvation. Guest completion stamps and resource ordering stay unchanged.
+  GPU timing is read in existing completion handlers; CPU commit time is now
+  labelled correctly rather than described as total GPU waiting/execution.
+  https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/3DDrawing/Conceptual/MTLBestPracticesGuide/CommandBuffers.html
+  https://developer.apple.com/documentation/metal/mtlcommandbuffer/gpustarttime
+- QEMU multi-threaded TCG documents global flushes and all lookup/direct-jump
+  invalidation requirements. Merely retaining old executable bytes is unsafe.
+  Henge has direct chained exits, literals, guest generations and shared slots;
+  a hot-region eviction allocator needs separate design and stress validation.
+  No partial cache-retention implementation is shipped as a completed speedup.
+  https://www.qemu.org/docs/master/devel/multi-thread-tcg.html
+- Apple's Virtualization framework targets macOS hosts. Its Mac VM acceleration
+  APIs cannot simply replace iPadOS Henge's software CPU emulation. UTM's iOS
+  documentation returned HTTP 403 during this review and was not used as evidence.
+  https://developer.apple.com/documentation/virtualization
+
+## Efficiency changes at unchanged FPS
+
+1. A local GIC PPI change re-arbitrates only the affected CPU, rather than every
+   virtual CPU. Shared interrupt changes retain the full scan. Generic virtual
+   and physical timer outputs share one sampled counter value, one lock when
+   changed, one local arbitration. Unchanged outputs retain a lock-free check.
+   Tests cover both timer levels, repeated updates, edge latching, another CPU's
+   routed SPI, and the existing real JIT timer IRQ guest program.
+2. Shared planar IOSurface references within one submitted Metal batch hash/sync
+   the exact same surface only once. Multiple texture views previously repeated
+   that CPU memory scan. Different objects and subsequent batches still check;
+   guest resource contents are stable for a submitted batch under Metal's
+   synchronization contract. Counters expose performed/skipped scans. This does
+   not cache mutable textures across batches. A production-helper sanitizer test
+   reduces 1000 repeated views to one check and tests aliases, epochs and wrap.
+3. GPU completion diagnostics record real execution duration, maximum and missing
+   timing. The sum is not wall-clock utilization: command buffers may overlap.
+   Existing session-only exact-byte shader library reuse remains, with limits
+   and retry semantics unchanged. No measured M4 FPS or wattage improvement is
+   inferred from correctness tests or from the synthetic repeated-view case.
+
+## Optional controls (off by default)
+
+Thermal display protection coalesces host presentation at 30 fps in serious heat
+and 20 fps in critical heat. Presentation limits are independent of Retina and
+use one deferred wake for a pending batch, none at idle; the newest frame wins.
+Late target callbacks are generation-cancelled. Recovery to a cooler state and
+settings are checked at most once per second while frames arrive. The guest may
+still render faster, so host pacing alone does not bound all guest graphics work.
+A new selectable 30 Hz guest mode can reduce guest animation work after restart;
+it is also opt-in. Neither is represented as a higher-current-FPS optimization.
+
+Tests exercise the production scheduling method with real dispatch queues: a
+1000-event burst becomes one deferred presentation, final work is retained,
+idle schedules no extra work and stale target/reconnect behavior is covered.
+The actual Metal shader pixel tests, cache tests and CPU/device regressions
+remain required. Physical M4 timing, power and panic reproduction remain open.
