+# Architecture and source audit
+
+Read against clean 0.2, current main and the starting custom source. The audit
+index lists current function locations and complete file hashes for the requested
+files and additional relevant files. Line numbers are navigation aids, not a
+claim that physical power was measured at that line.
+
+## Boundaries
+
+1. iPadOS schedules Henge's host threads and supplies JIT/debug/mapping, Mach,
+   CoreAudio, sockets/files, Metal and Core Animation. It sees Henge, not an
+   independently scheduled native macOS game. No iOS hypervisor is used.
+2. core/dispatch.c enters translated blocks; core/translate.c copies ordinary
+   ARM64 work and rewrites privileged/system, memory and control operations.
+   Host x6/x7/x15/x18/x28 reservations force selected guest spills. PC-relative
+   materialization, branch/return caches and software TLB checks are necessary
+   isolation overhead even though M4 understands ARM64.
+3. core/mmu.c walks guest page tables and maintains per-vCPU TLB/context/ASID
+   state. Guest loads/stores use inline tags/addends or helpers; MMIO, atomics,
+   cross-page access, permissions and code writes need special handling.
+4. core/machine.c owns RAM/MMIO, translation publication/invalidation, request/
+   acknowledgment synchronization and CPU wake primitives. Single-instruction
+   branch patches are atomic; code-page generations and cross-page link index
+   must remain coherent across CPUs. Generated executable mappings are not a
+   persistent disk cache.
+5. host/vm.c owns the virtual board, CPU threads and ticker. Clean 0.2 has an
+   unconditional lifetime 1ms all-CPU kicker. Prior custom cancels by generation,
+   condition-waits if every CPU is parked, and only marks active CPUs. This
+   candidate retains that production policy, adding an opt-in timer filter.
+6. devices/vmapple.c tick polls CPU0 devices under the device lock; other CPUs
+   update their own generic timers. Timer registers now have a timer-only
+   callback. devices/timer.c drives the virtual/physical PPIs through gicv3.c;
+   routed shared interrupts still need global arbitration.
+7. virtio_blk workers wait on condition variables; completion wakes CPU0. Disk
+   barrier/flush ordering is meaningful. virtio_snd drains a ring at the audio
+   rate, supplies completion/latency and wakes on consumed data. Network slirp
+   uses poll, deadlines and a pipe with explicit arrivals. No arbitrary polling
+   interval/QoS increases are made for these latency-sensitive paths.
+8. Guest Metal/WindowServer encode objects/resources and serialized calls into
+   guest memory. pvg_lite.c drains channels, handles barriers/stamps/mapping and
+   routes commands to pvg_metal.m's serial worker. Object references and pages
+   are read through the guest mapping model, not normal host pointers.
+9. pvg_metal.m reconstructs native Metal buffers/textures/libraries/pipelines,
+   replays render/compute/blit records, encodes transfers when either copy is
+   stale, commits asynchronously and returns guest stamps on GPU completion.
+   It does not provide all of Metal as a native passthrough framework. Existing
+   SPI descriptor deserialization is retained, not expanded by this project.
+10. Multi-plane IOSurfaces can be shared across guest processes. Exact physical
+    page/layout identity now precedes host IOSurface allocation. Content copying
+    still uses the inherited sampled detector; known unsampled-change limitation
+    is reproduced and explicitly not resolved by the registry optimization.
+11. showOnLayer/presentLoop choose a shared-memory scanout texture, composite
+    screen and premultiplied cursor via native Metal, and present CAMetalDrawable.
+    No ordinary CPU full-frame readback is introduced. The presentation worker
+    is separate from guest GPU submission, so nextDrawable need not block it.
+12. BootDisplay.swift's CGImage copy is for the early firmware framebuffer; it
+    stops after GPU frames begin. DisplayRouter.swift selects internal/external
+    layers and re-presents on reattachment. Native pixel dimensions, colourspace
+    selection and guest physical-display dimensions are unchanged.
+
+## Necessary demand versus plausible waste
+
+Guest game simulation, guest OS services, true pixel shading, texture transfers
+that actually changed, audio samples and genuine interrupts are useful work.
+Software memory translation, reserved-register spills and JIT compilation are
+architectural costs that need measured redesign rather than removal of checks.
+Redundant IOSurface allocation, incidental device polls from timer MSRs, unused
+JIT tail space and unchanged homepage JPEG writes are removable work identified
+in source. Their individual device-energy significance is not established.
+
+The seven-million-draw session demonstrates substantial graphics activity and
+zero *reported* replay failures, not complete visual compatibility or GPU
+utilization. Serious thermal state is observed; CPU/GPU watts and physical
+silicon temperature are not present in those logs.
