+Fit-mode correction for the Duo, 8 November 2026
+
+The user confirmed Fit this iPhone is selected and supplied a 1280x900 image
+with slim top/bottom bars. The image alone does not establish the native panel
+dimensions or the exact guest mode. It does establish a visible mismatch.
+
+Previously Fit stored a size computed from UIScreen.main.bounds, rounded guest
+height to eight pixels and capped it at 1440. The stored size remained fixed
+even if the app window or folding state changed. SessionView fit the display
+without stretching, so mismatched shapes produced black bars. The host and
+guest must agree on the mode; replacing aspect fit with stretch/crop would hide
+content or distort it.
+
+Fit is now an explicit optional persisted selection. New installs and setting
+changes set it; legacy 1920-wide, eight-aligned tall profiles are recognized
+using the old Fit convention. Explicit fixed, Retina, 16:9 and performance
+choices are retained. Manually edited old profiles that happen to use the exact
+legacy Fit convention are ambiguous; setting fitScreen=false preserves their
+fixed dimensions. No guest disks or installed macOS contents are rewritten.
+
+At VM launch, SessionView resolves Fit using the measured full viewport. The
+1920-pixel width and the previous 1080-height floor for wide phones are retained.
+Tall-window heights use nearest-pixel precision with no 1440 cap. Row alignment
+does not require eight-pixel-high textures. A 1280x900 viewport therefore chooses
+1920x1350 instead of the illustrative older 1920x1320 mode, eliminating its
+ten-pixel top/bottom bars mathematically. Arbitrary ratios can retain less than
+one guest pixel of quantization; the implementation does not promise exact
+rational ratios for every possible window size.
+
+The chosen guest mode is held throughout the session and shared by the display,
+pointer layout, minimap, external presentation and previews. Fold/rotation while
+the VM is running therefore fits the existing guest mode; it does not resize
+the guest or silently stretch it. Restart in the desired folding state to choose
+a new Fit mode. Colour handling, shader output, refresh rate, CPU/RAM and Retina
+mode are unchanged. Correcting Fit may add necessary vertical pixels; it is not
+a thermal-efficiency benchmark or a reduced-resolution performance change.
+
+New launch logs: display-viewport (window/layer/scale) and screen-fit (selection,
+measured viewport, stored dimensions, actual guest dimensions/aspect). These
+are event/launch logs, not a recurring poll.
+
+Validation added: production Swift size/policy tests for the reported proportions,
+portrait normalization, nearby ratios, a broad size sweep, square windows,
+invalid/nonfinite inputs, extreme finite inputs, legacy migration and explicit
+mode persistence. Native Metal tests now verify all pixels and the last row
+of a 1920x1350 frame using both bundled and fallback production shaders, while
+retaining the existing colour/half-float/cursor tests. iOS build and actual
+device viewport verification remain required; no physical Duo test is claimed.
+
+The first native validation passed the new full-frame Metal pixel check, then
+stopped because the standalone Foundation-only Swift fixture could not resolve
+CGSize.zero. The fixture now uses an explicit zero-size initializer; production
+sizing was unchanged by that test portability repair.
