@@ -0,0 +1,92 @@
+# Apple platform research and gaming integration
+
+Apple pages were fetched on 8 November 2026; raw responses are saved by the
+investigation under artifacts/thermal-engineering/research. These documents
+are the evidence, rather than guesses about a future/current OS version.
+
+## Game Mode
+
+Apple Support 105118 says Game Mode gives highest-priority CPU/GPU access,
+reduces background activity and doubles Bluetooth sampling; it requires a
+supported game on iPadOS 18+. Fullscreen and Game Overlay are relevant to the
+*host*. Guest fullscreen/macOS Game Mode is not host activation.
+https://support.apple.com/en-gb/105118
+
+Current LSSupportsGameMode documentation lists iOS/iPadOS 18.6+ and macOS 26+.
+The Boolean YES makes Game Mode available; omission might prevent activation.
+This is a documented metadata opt-in, not a provisioning entitlement, a direct
+activation API or proof of recognition for a sideloaded general-purpose emulator.
+No evidence from these pages establishes that all signing routes or emulator
+categories are recognized identically. No system classification is spoofed.
+https://developer.apple.com/documentation/bundleresources/information-property-list/lssupportsgamemode
+
+Normal metadata remains unchanged. tools/configure_gaming_experiments.py can
+insert only that Boolean for a separate build; workflow_dispatch game_mode=true
+provides this isolated artifact. Compare Game Overlay/Game Mode on and off on
+the same signed build where iPadOS offers it. Activation, background competition,
+Bluetooth latency and energy effects remain unverified. More resource access
+can increase heat. Not a default thermal optimization.
+
+## Sustained execution
+
+The current entitlement page explicitly says it **limits burst performance** and
+avoids higher performance levels on applicable devices. It lists iOS/iPadOS 17+
+and macOS 15+, but does not prove that this exact M4/signing route supports it.
+No authorizing mobile provisioning profile is available in this environment.
+Normal, personal-team and helper entitlement files are unchanged.
+https://developer.apple.com/documentation/bundleresources/entitlements/com.apple.developer.sustained-execution
+
+The build tool provides an isolated path that first decodes a supplied signed
+profile, checks bundle matching, authorization of sustained execution and every
+existing entitlement, and writes a *different* entitlement file. Without such
+a profile it refuses. Fixture authorization tests are not actual Apple approval,
+signing, installation or an AMFI launch check. An ad-hoc signature alone proves
+none of those. Never enable by default without equivalent throughput/latency
+and lower-energy measurements. No sustained-execution IPA was built here.
+
+## Scheduling and hardware
+
+QoS is a system scheduling hint, not a documented public M4 P-core/E-core affinity
+control. Existing guest user-initiated QoS is retained, as are interactive GPU
+submission/presentation and CoreAudio scheduling. Background preview/log work
+stays utility. Core residency, migration and frequency require device Instruments
+or supported system profiling; no per-core claim is fabricated.
+https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/Performance/Conceptual/EnergyGuide-iOS/PrioritizeWorkWithQoS.html
+
+The Virtualization framework documentation targets macOS hosts, so the fact that
+both guest and host are ARM64 does not expose Mac hardware VM APIs to an iPad app.
+https://developer.apple.com/documentation/virtualization
+
+ProMotion supports dynamic refresh and Core Animation arbitrates physical output;
+a preferred rate is not a promise that every frame reaches the panel. Keep the
+selected 60/120Hz guest mode and native dimensions. No downscaling, MetalFX,
+brightness change, thermal override or hidden animation reduction is used.
+https://developer.apple.com/documentation/quartzcore/optimizing-iphone-and-ipad-apps-to-support-promotion-displays
+
+## Metal, memory and measurement
+
+Load/store choices can avoid memory traffic, but the emulator must honor guest
+render-pass semantics, resolve actions and persistence. It cannot discard colour,
+depth or stencil simply because a native game sample does. Existing presenter
+full-overwrite DontCare/Store is retained; guest settings are not simplified.
+https://developer.apple.com/documentation/metal/setting-load-and-store-actions/
+
+CPU/GPU work overlaps; streaming, shader creation and queue stalls need separate
+measurement. A sum of command-buffer durations is not GPU-active utilization.
+Presentation intervals also include cursor updates and idle content, so they
+are not a game's internal FPS. Thermal state is not degrees Celsius, and wakeups
+are not watts. Current guidance emphasizes observe, isolate, diagnose, then tune.
+https://developer.apple.com/documentation/metal/improving-your-games-graphics-performance-and-settings
+https://developer.apple.com/videos/play/meet-with-apple/242/
+https://developer.apple.com/documentation/metal/mtlcommandbuffer/gpustarttime
+
+Use Time Profiler/thread states, Power Profiler, Game Performance Overview,
+Metal System Trace, Metal HUD and GPU capture/counters on the physical device
+where available. Availability/permissions/counter families vary; unsupported
+measurements must remain unavailable. Record Instruments/Xcode version and
+whether the trace itself is enabled during a trial. Do not take sampled-profile
+power as an uninstrumented energy baseline.
+
+QEMU's multi-threaded translation documentation helps identify invalidation and
+cross-CPU lifetime obligations, not a drop-in architecture or iOS hardware VM.
+https://www.qemu.org/docs/master/devel/multi-thread-tcg.html
